{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region=Auckland}}
'''Pakuranga''' is a New Zealand Parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]]. It gave the [[Social Credit Party (New Zealand)|Social Credit Party]] one of its few MPs when [[Neil Morrison]] held the seat from 1984 to 1987, but otherwise the electorate seat has been held by the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] since 1972. 
Its current MP is [[Simeon Brown]] who has held the electorate since the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]].

==Population centres==
Pakuranga is one of 64 general electorates used in New Zealand general elections. It covers part of [[East Auckland]] around the eponymous suburb of [[Pakuranga]]. Other population centres include [[Farm Cove, New Zealand|Farm Cove]], [[Half Moon Bay, Auckland|Half Moon Bay]], [[Bucklands Beach]], parts of [[Highland Park, New Zealand|Highland Park]] and parts of [[Howick, New Zealand|Howick]].<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/pakuranga-electorate-profile/ |title=Pakuranga – Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 23 September 2025}}</ref>

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Pakuranga.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The boundaries of the Pakuranga electorate were last adjusted in the 2007 redistribution. Initially, the Representation Commission proposed substantial changes, but after hearing objections mainly about Pakuranga, {{NZ electorate link|Manukau East}}, and {{NZ electorate link|Manurewa}}, the shape of the Pakuranga electorate reverted to almost the boundaries prior to the review.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2007 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |pages=8, 13 |date=14 September 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190123193826/https://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |archive-date=23 January 2019 |url-status=dead }}</ref> No boundary adjustments were undertaken in the 2013/14 and 2020 redistributions.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=9 |date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate would gain the outer [[Auckland]] suburbs of [[Cockle Bay, New Zealand|Cockle Bay]] and [[Shelly Park]] from {{NZ electorate link|Botany}}.<ref>{{cite web |date=8 August 2025 |title=Electorate boundaries finalised |url=https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2025/electorate-boundaries-finalised/// |work=Elections NZ}}</ref>

==History==
Pakuranga was first contested at the {{NZ election link|1963}}, and won by future [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] frontbencher [[Bob Tizard]]. It was captured by National in {{NZ election link year|1972}}, and has stayed with National since, save for a brief interlude  – the [[New Zealand Party]]'s presence on the ballot paper in {{NZ election link year|1984}} split the centre-right vote and handed the seat to [[Social Credit Party (New Zealand)|Social Credit]]'s [[Neil Morrison]].

With the introduction of [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) representation in 1996 the seat was expanded to absorb most of the neighbouring seat of [[Howick (New Zealand electorate)|Howick]]. Its boundaries have remained largely unchanged since; an attempt in 2007 to resurrect a seat around Howick by pulling the Pakuranga boundaries across the [[Tamaki River]] and adding [[Panmure, New Zealand|Panmure]], [[Point England]] and [[Glen Innes, New Zealand|Glen Innes]] from Auckland City was abandoned in the face of strenuous local objection. Instead, Howick was renamed [[Botany (New Zealand electorate)|Botany]] and centred on the rapid-growth areas of [[Flat Bush]], [[Botany Downs]] and [[Dannemora, New Zealand|Dannemora]].

Pakuranga is considered a safe National seat, with [[Maurice Williamson]] being easily re-elected at every election to 2014 after ousting Morrison in {{NZ election link year|1987}}.<ref>{{cite web |title=Hon Maurice Williamson |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/current/51MP1151/williamson-hon-maurice |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=4 October 2014}}</ref> In the {{NZ election link|2014}}, Williamson had a majority of 12,867 votes over his nearest challenger, Barry Kirker of the Labour Party.<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2014/electorate-38.html |title=Official Count Results – Pakuranga |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=4 October 2014 |date=4 October 2014}}</ref>

Williamson announced in July 2016 that he would not stand for Parliament at the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]]<ref>{{cite news |title=National and Pakuranga MP Maurice Williamson to leave Parliament |first=Nicholas |last=Jones |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11681442 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=26 July 2016 |access-date=27 July 2016}}</ref> and the seat was won by [[Simeon Brown]], retaining it for the National Party.

[[Simeon Brown]] has confirmed he is standing for the Pakuranga electorate in the [[2026 New Zealand general election]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Simeon Says — 29 November 2025 |url=https://www.national.org.nz/news/251129-simeonbrown |website=National.org.nz |publisher=National Party |access-date=19 May 2026}}</ref> Labour has selected Campbell Matthews as their candidate.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cooke |first1=Henry |title=Can the coalition go the distance? |url=https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/360996458/can-coalition-go-distance |access-date=19 May 2026 |agency=The Post |date=April 30, 2026}}</ref>

